T Six-axis K'NEX robot arm, cable-driven from an operator lever stand
# U = 37.5 mm, z up, desk at z = 0. The arm itself is builds/robot_arm.knx unchanged: six slider-crank
# axes, each a rod sliding through guide hubs, a yoke, a coupler link and a crank pin.
#
# What is different here is the DRIVE. Nothing pushes on a slider any more. Every axis is pulled by a
# string (Y) that runs from a lever on an anchored operator stand, over guide hubs, to the far cap of
# that axis's slider rod. A string can only pull, so each axis has an antagonist: gravity for the
# elbow, wrist and gripper, rubber bands for yaw, roll and the boom. The operator's hands (F lines)
# touch nothing but the six lever handles.
#
# The routing rule that makes it work: EVERY guide sits ON the axis of the joint it crosses, so the
# routed length of a cable does not change when an upstream joint moves. YCAP is on the yaw axis,
# SGY on the shoulder axis, EL on the elbow axis, FK on the wrist-pitch axis, WA on the roll axis.
! base deck: a 4U square with a rear extension for ballast. The outer nodes are 3D pairs, because a
! horizontal deck rod and a vertical leg are never coplanar.
C P1 B7 2,2,1.5 z -x
C P1v B7 2,2,1.5 x -z
C P2 B7 2,-2,1.5 z y
C P2v B7 2,-2,1.5 x -z
C P3 W8 -2,-2,1.5
C P4 W8 -2,2,1.5
C P5 W8 -2,0,1.5
C GB3 W8 -3,-2,1.5
C R1 B7 -4,-2,1.5 z x
C R1v B7 -4,-2,1.5 x -z
C R2 B7 -4,2,1.5 z -y
C R2v B7 -4,2,1.5 x -z
C R3 W8 -4,0,1.5
R P1 P2
R P2 P3
R P4 P1
R P3 P5
R P5 P4
R P3 GB3
R GB3 R1
R P5 R3
R P4 R2
R R1 R3
R R3 R2
R R1 P5
R P5 R2
! the centre node is a 3D pair too: the deck rods lie in its plane, the yaw axle stands on its partner
C BC B7 0,0,1.5 z x+y
C BCV B7 0,0,1.5 y z
R BC P1
R BC P2
R BC P3
R BC P4
R BC P5
! feet: the only parts that touch the desk
C FT1 W8 2,2,0.5 x y
C FT2 W8 2,-2,0.5 x y
C FT5 W8 -4,-2,0.5 x y
C FT6 W8 -4,2,0.5 x y
R FT1 P1v
R FT2 P2v
R FT5 R1v
R FT6 R2v
! ballast at the back, so the reach of the arm does not tip it
W ballastL R1 mass=400
W ballastR R2 mass=400
! AXIS 1 bearing: a red rod standing on the centre pair, capped at the top
R BCV 0,0,5.5 red
C YCAP D1 0,0,5.5 y -z
! the column: a braced box riding two hubs on that axle. The spacers under the lower hub turn the
! bearing into a thrust bearing so the column cannot slide down the axle.
H Y1 W8 0,0,2.5
H Y2 W8 0,0,4.5
P 0,0,2.3 silver
P 0,0,2.7 silver
C CA B7 1,1,2.5 z -x
C CAv B7 1,1,2.5 x z
C CB B7 1,-1,2.5 z y
C CBv B7 1,-1,2.5 -y z
C CC B7 -1,-1,2.5 z x
C CCv B7 -1,-1,2.5 -x z
C CD B7 -1,1,2.5 z -y
C CDv B7 -1,1,2.5 y z
R Y1 CA
R Y1 CB
R Y1 CC
R Y1 CD
R CA CB
R CB CC
R CC CD
R CD CA
C CA2 B7 1,1,4.5 z -x
C CA2v B7 1,1,4.5 y -z
C CB2 B7 1,-1,4.5 z y
C CB2v B7 1,-1,4.5 x -z
C CC2 B7 -1,-1,4.5 z x-y
C CC2v B7 -1,-1,4.5 -y -z
C CD2 B7 -1,1,4.5 z -y
C CD2v B7 -1,1,4.5 -x -z
R Y2 CA2
R Y2 CB2
R Y2 CC2
R Y2 CD2
R CA2 CB2
R CB2 CC2
R CC2 CD2
R CD2 CA2
! four uprights and four face diagonals, one starting and one landing at every corner
R CAv CA2v
R CBv CB2v
R CCv CC2v
R CDv CD2v
R CAv CB2v
R CBv CC2v
R CCv CD2v
R CDv CA2v
! AXIS 1 crank: a triangulated arm off the column top reaching 2U behind the axle, with a pin
! hanging from its tip. The coupler runs parallel to the slide, which is where the torque comes from.
C CK2 B7 0,-2,4.5 z x
C CK2v B7 0,-2,4.5 x -z
R CB2 CK2
R CC2 CK2
C CKC D1 0,-2,3.5 x z
R CK2v CKC
H CY2 W8 0,-2,4
P 0,-2,3.8 silver
P 0,-2,4.2 silver
! AXIS 1 actuator: a red rod sliding along x at y=-2, z=2.5, with its yoke pin standing up to z=4.5
H CY1 W8 -2,-2,4
P -2,-2,3.8 silver
P -2,-2,4.2 silver
R CY1 CY2
C YK1 R3 -2,-2,2.5 y -x
C PC1 D1 -2,-2,4.5 x -z
R YK1 PC1
C AC1 D1 -6,-2,2.5 y x
R YK1 AC1
H GH1 W8 -3,-2,2.5
H GH2 W8 -4,-2,2.5
R GH1 GB3
R GH2 R1v
! opposed centring bands
E yawA1 AC1 R1 rest=68 k=0.148
E yawA2 AC1 R1 rest=68 k=0.148
E yawB1 YK1 GB3 rest=30 k=0.08
E yawB2 YK1 GB3 rest=30 k=0.08
! AXIS 2 shoulder: a hub on the front rail of the column top. The upper arm is a flat triangle in the
! plane y=0; the crank is the arm itself, with the pin 2U out from the bearing.
H SH W8 1,0,4.5
P 1,-0.2,4.5 silver
P 1,0.2,4.5 silver
C MIDU W8 2,0,4.5
C UA B7 3,0,4.5 y -x
C UAv B7 3,0,4.5 z -y
C EL B7 3,0,6.5 y -z
C ELv B7 3,0,6.5 z y
R SH MIDU
R MIDU UA
R UA EL
R SH EL
! crank pin on the upper arm, coupler down and back to the slider
C SCC D1 3,-2,4.5 x y
R UAv SCC
H SCP W8 3,-1,4.5
P 3,-0.8,4.5 silver
P 3,-1.2,4.5 silver
H SAP W8 1,-1,6.5
P 1,-0.8,6.5 silver
P 1,-1.2,6.5 silver
R SCP SAP
! AXIS 2 actuator: a red rod sliding vertically at x=1, y=-2, guided by a hub braced into the
! horizontal triangle of the column top
C YK2 R3 1,-2,6.5 x -z
C PC2 D1 1,0,6.5 x -y
R YK2 PC2
C AS1 D1 1,-2,2.5 x z
R YK2 AS1
H GS1 W8 1,-2,4.5
R GS1 CB2
R GS1 CK2
E shoA YK2 CB2 rest=70 k=0.02
E shoB1 AS1 CB2 rest=50 k=0.04
! AXIS 3 elbow: axle cantilevered off the elbow node, forearm as an A-frame in the plane y=1
C ECAP D1 3,2,6.5 x -y
R ELv ECAP
H FH W8 3,1,6.5
P 3,0.8,6.5 silver
P 3,1.2,6.5 silver
C FK B7 5,1,6.5 y -x-z
C FKv B7 5,1,6.5 z y
C FB W8 4,1,5.5
R FH FK
R FH FB
R FB FK
! crank pin at the far end of the forearm, coupler back and down to the slider
C ECC D1 5,3,6.5 x -y
R FKv ECC
H ECP W8 5,2,6.5
P 5,1.8,6.5 silver
P 5,2.2,6.5 silver
H EAP W8 3,2,4.5
P 3,1.8,4.5 silver
P 3,2.2,4.5 silver
R ECP EAP
! AXIS 3 actuator: a red rod sliding along x at y=3, z=4.5, guided off a bracket on the upper arm
C YK3 R3 3,3,4.5 z -x
C PC3 D1 3,1,4.5 x y
R YK3 PC3
C AE1 D1 -1,3,4.5 y x
R YK3 AE1
H GE1 W8 2,3,4.5
C BRB W8 2,1,4.5
R GE1 BRB
R BRB MIDU
R BRB UAv
E elbA YK3 GE1 rest=10 k=0.063
E elbB AE1 GE1 rest=90 k=0.02
! mast on the forearm, carrying the two guides of the wrist-pitch actuator
C MB W8 1,1,6.5
C MA B7 2,1,7.5 y x-z
C MAv B7 2,1,7.5 z y
C MD B7 1,1,7.5 y -z
C MDv B7 1,1,7.5 z y
R FH MB
R FH MA
R MB MA
R MB MD
R MA MD
! AXIS 4 wrist pitch: axle cantilevered off the forearm elbow node, wrist link is a triangle at y=0
C WCAP R3 5,-1,6.5 x y
R FKv WCAP
H WH W8 5,0,6.5
P 5,-0.2,6.5 silver
P 5,0.2,6.5 silver
C WT B7 6,0,7.5 y -x-z
C WTv B7 6,0,7.5 z y
C WA W8 7,0,6.5
R WH WT
R WT WA
R WH WA
C W4CC D1 6,2,7.5 x -y
R WTv W4CC
H W4C W8 6,1,7.5
P 6,0.8,7.5 silver
P 6,1.2,7.5 silver
H W4A W8 4,1,7.5
P 4,0.8,7.5 silver
P 4,1.2,7.5 silver
R W4A W4C
C YK4 R3 4,2,7.5 z -x
C PC4 D1 4,0,7.5 x y
R YK4 PC4
C AE4 D1 0,2,7.5 y x
R YK4 AE4
H GW1 W8 2,2,7.5
H GW2 W8 1,2,7.5
R GW1 MAv
R GW2 MDv
E wriA1 YK4 GW1 rest=26 k=0.04
E wriB AE4 GW1 rest=45 k=0.02
! AXIS 5 wrist roll: the hand rides a hub on a shaft along the arm's own axis
C RCAP R3 9,0,6.5 y -z
R WA RCAP
H RH W8 8,0,6.5
P 7.8,0,6.5 silver
P 8.2,0,6.5 silver
C HU W8 8,0,7.5 x -z
C HL B7 8,0,5.5 x y+z
C HLv B7 8,0,5.5 z -x
C HR W8 8,1,6.5
C HB B7 8,-1,6.5 x y-z
C HBv B7 8,-1,6.5 z x
R RH HU
R RH HL
R RH HR
R RH HB
R HU HR
R HR HL
R HL HB
R HB HU
! roll crank: a pin along the arm axis, 1U below it, and a coupler across in y
C R5CC D1 6,0,5.5 y x
R HLv R5CC
H R5C W8 7,0,5.5
P 6.8,0,5.5 silver
P 7.2,0,5.5 silver
H R5A W8 7,2,5.5
P 6.8,2,5.5 silver
P 7.2,2,5.5 silver
R R5C R5A
C YK5 R3 5,2,5.5 z -y
C PC5 D1 9,2,5.5 y -x
R YK5 PC5
C AE5 D1 5,-2,5.5 x y
R YK5 AE5
H GR1 W8 5,0,5.5
R GR1 WH
E rolA YK5 GR1 rest=55
E rolB AE5 GR1 rest=69 k=0.1
! AXIS 6 gripper: one jaw pivoting on an axle through the top of the hand, closing past a fixed anvil
C JAC D1 8,2,7.5 x -y
R HU JAC
H JH W8 8,1,7.5
P 8,0.8,7.5 silver
P 8,1.2,7.5 silver
C JT B7 10,1,7.5 y -x
C JTv B7 10,1,7.5 z -y
C JD R3 10,1,5.5 y -x+z
R JH JT
R JT JD
R JH JD
C JF W8 10,0,5.5
R HLv JF
! gripper crank pin on the jaw, coupler up to the slider
C G6CC D1 10,-1,7.5 x y
R JTv G6CC
H G6C W8 10,0,7.5
P 10,-0.2,7.5 silver
P 10,0.2,7.5 silver
H G6A W8 11,0,8.5
P 11,-0.2,8.5 silver
P 11,0.2,8.5 silver
R G6C G6A
C YK6 R3 11,-1,8.5 x -z
C PC6 D1 11,1,8.5 x -y
R YK6 PC6
C AE6 D1 11,-1,4.5 x z
R YK6 AE6
H GG1 W8 11,-1,6.5
C GB0 W8 9,-1,6.5
R GG1 GB0
R GB0 HBv
E griA YK6 GG1 rest=55
E griB AE6 GG1 rest=55
! OPERATOR STAND, deck: an 8U x 2U console on the desk behind the machine, four feet, the back rail
! carrying the diagonals so every cell is a triangle. Anchored (A) because a lever is a hand pushing
! on the world: without the anchor the console would ride away every time a lever is used.
C KB4n  P4 -9,-4,1.5 z x
C KB4nv B7 -9,-4,1.5 x -z
C KB2n  W8 -9,-2,1.5 z x
C KB0   W8 -9,0,1.5 z x
C KB2p  W8 -9,2,1.5 z x
C KB4p  P4 -9,4,1.5 z -y
C KB4pv B7 -9,4,1.5 x -z
C KF4n  P4 -7,-4,1.5 z y
C KF4nv B7 -7,-4,1.5 x -z
C KF2n  W8 -7,-2,1.5 z y
C KF0   B7 -7,0,1.5 z y
C KF0v  B7 -7,0,1.5 x -z
C KF2p  W8 -7,2,1.5 z y
C KF4p  P4 -7,4,1.5 z -x
C KF4pv B7 -7,4,1.5 x -z
R KB4n KB2n
R KB2n KB0
R KB0 KB2p
R KB2p KB4p
R KF4n KF2n
R KF2n KF0
R KF0 KF2p
R KF2p KF4p
R KB4n KF4n
R KB2n KF2n
R KB0 KF0
R KB2p KF2p
R KB4p KF4p
R KB4n KF2n
R KB2n KF0
R KB0 KF2p
R KB2p KF4p
C KFTa W8 -9,-4,0.5 x y
C KFTb W8 -9,4,0.5 x y
C KFTc W8 -7,-4,0.5 x y
C KFTd W8 -7,4,0.5 x y
R KFTa KB4nv
R KFTb KB4pv
R KFTc KF4nv
R KFTd KF4pv
A KB0
! OPERATOR STAND, front truss: three posts carry a fairlead rail at z=2.5 and the lever axle at 3.5.
! The rail is a chain of green rods so every guide node is a rigid part of the console and cannot
! slide or spin under cable load; only the six lever bearings on the axle above it are free.
C KR4n W8 -7,-4,2.5 x z
C KRn3 W8 -7,-3,2.5 x z
C KRn2 W8 -7,-2,2.5 x z
C KRn1 W8 -7,-1,2.5 x z
C KR0  W8 -7,0,2.5 x z
C KRp1 W8 -7,1,2.5 x z
C KRp2 W8 -7,2,2.5 x z
C KRp3 W8 -7,3,2.5 x z
C KR4p W8 -7,4,2.5 x z
R KF4nv KR4n
R KF0v KR0
R KF4pv KR4p
R KR4n KRn3
R KRn3 KRn2
R KRn2 KRn1
R KRn1 KR0
R KR0 KRp1
R KRp1 KRp2
R KRp2 KRp3
R KRp3 KR4p
C KA4n W8 -7,-4,3.5 x z
C KA0  W8 -7,0,3.5 x z
C KA4p W8 -7,4,3.5 x z
R KR4n KA4n
R KR0 KA0
R KR4p KA4p
R KA4n KA0
R KA0 KA4p
R KRn3 KA4n
R KRn1 KA0
R KRp1 KA0
R KRp3 KA4p
R KRn3 KF4nv
R KRn1 KF0v
R KRp1 KF0v
R KRp3 KF4pv
! OPERATOR STAND, the lever bank: six hubs on the axle, each its own bearing, each with three arms.
! Handle 4U up (the hand goes here and nowhere else), cable arm 1.4U back-and-down (the string), and
! return arm 1U forward carrying a band down to the deck. The band holds the lever forward against
! its cable, so at rest every cable is taut and the arm's pose is defined by the console, not by luck.
! Push a handle forward and its cable arm swings away from the fairlead: the cable shortens on the
! machine side and drives. Pull the handle back and the cable pays out and the axis's antagonist wins.
H LBM W8 -7,-3,3.5 z
P -7,-3.2,3.5 silver
P -7,-2.8,3.5 silver
C LBMh D1 -7,-3,7.5 y -z
C LBMc D1 -8,-3,2.5 y x+z
C LBMr D1 -6,-3,3.5 y -x
R LBM LBMh
R LBM LBMc
R LBM LBMr
H LSW W8 -7,-2,3.5 z
P -7,-2.2,3.5 silver
P -7,-1.8,3.5 silver
C LSWh D1 -7,-2,7.5 y -z
C LSWc D1 -8,-2,2.5 y x+z
C LSWr D1 -6,-2,3.5 y -x
R LSW LSWh
R LSW LSWc
R LSW LSWr
H LST W8 -7,-1,3.5 z
P -7,-1.2,3.5 silver
P -7,-0.8,3.5 silver
C LSTh D1 -7,-1,7.5 y -z
C LSTc D1 -8,-1,2.5 y x+z
C LSTr D1 -6,-1,3.5 y -x
R LST LSTh
R LST LSTc
R LST LSTr
H LWR W8 -7,1,3.5 z
P -7,0.8,3.5 silver
P -7,1.2,3.5 silver
C LWRh D1 -7,1,7.5 y -z
C LWRc D1 -8,1,2.5 y x+z
C LWRr D1 -6,1,3.5 y -x
R LWR LWRh
R LWR LWRc
R LWR LWRr
H LGR W8 -7,2,3.5 z
P -7,1.8,3.5 silver
P -7,2.2,3.5 silver
C LGRh D1 -7,2,7.5 y -z
C LGRc D1 -8,2,2.5 y x+z
C LGRr D1 -6,2,3.5 y -x
R LGR LGRh
R LGR LGRc
R LGR LGRr
! the roll control is not a lever but a crank: a 2U arm to a hub-height wheel node and a grip along
! the axle, so the operator's hand turns it round instead of pushing it fore and aft.
H LRL W8 -7,3,3.5 z
P -7,2.8,3.5 silver
P -7,3.2,3.5 silver
C LRLw  B7 -7,3,5.5 y -z
C LRLwv B7 -7,3,5.5 z y
C LRLg  D1 -7,4,5.5 z -y
C LRLc D1 -8,3,2.5 y x+z
C LRLr D1 -6,3,3.5 y -x
R LRL LRLw
R LRLwv LRLg
R LRL LRLc
R LRL LRLr
! lever return bands: each pulls the forward arm down onto the deck, which is +torque about the axle
! and therefore holds the handle forward and the cable taut. This is the operator's "feel": the band
! is what he pushes against, and what puts the lever back when he lets go.
E rbBM LBMr KF4n rest=55 k=0.05
E rbSW LSWr KF2n rest=50 k=0.05
E rbST LSTr KF0  rest=55 k=0.05
E rbWR LWRr KF0  rest=55 k=0.05
E rbGR LGRr KF2p rest=50 k=0.05
E rbRL LRLr KF4p rest=55 k=0.05
! the operator's hands. Nothing else in this model has an F on it: no finger touches a slider, a
! crank or a joint. 0.01 N declared, so --press "boom"=100 is a 1 N push on that handle.
F LBMh 0.01,0,0 boom
F LSWh 0.01,0,0 swing
F LSTh 0.01,0,0 stick
F LWRh 0.01,0,0 wrist
F LGRh 0.01,0,0 grip
F LRLg 0.01,0,0 roll
! SGY: the only new guide the machine needs. It hangs 1U off the column top ring at (1,2,4.5), which
! is a point ON the shoulder axis line (x=1, z=4.5, any y). Four cables turn here and none of them
! changes length when the shoulder pitches. YCAP, the cap on top of the yaw axle at (0,0,5.5), does
! the same job for the swing: it is on the yaw axis, so the whole cable loom passes through the slew
! centre exactly as it does on a real machine.
C SGY R3 1,2,4.5 x z
R CA2 SGY
! BG2: a spacer-parked hub riding the base's front rail directly under the boom slider's bottom cap.
! It is a real pulley - it spins on the rod - and its only job is to turn the boom cable through 90
! degrees so the pull on the slider is exactly vertical. Before it existed the cable left AS1 at 45
! degrees to the nearest base node and the horizontal half of that pull yawed the whole column: the
! boom lever swung the machine 1.7 degrees every time it was used.
H BG2 W8 1,-2,1.5 z
P 0.8,-2,1.5 silver
P 1.2,-2,1.5 silver
! THE CABLE LOOM. Each string runs lever arm -> fairlead on the console -> guides on the joint axes
! -> the far cap of that axis's slider rod. rest = the drawn length, so at the drawn pose every cable
! is exactly taut and the console is the position reference for the whole arm.
Y cabSwing LSWc AC1 via=KRn2 slack=3
Y cabBoom  LBMc AS1 via=KRn3,R1,BG2 slack=3
Y cabStick LSTc AE1 via=KRn1,YCAP,SGY slack=3
Y cabWrist LWRc AE4 via=KRp1,YCAP,SGY,EL slack=3
Y cabRoll  LRLc AE5 via=KRp3,YCAP,SGY,EL,FK slack=3
Y cabGrip  LGRc AE6 via=KRp2,YCAP,SGY,EL,FK,WA slack=3
! GRAVITY BALANCE. Three masts, each standing 2U directly above a pitch axis on the link BELOW it,
! and a bundle of near-zero-rest-length rubber bands from the mast top to a point 2U out along the
! link ABOVE it. A band whose rest length is almost zero pulls with a force proportional to its own
! length, and the torque it makes about the pivot is then k*h*r*cos(theta): the same cos(theta) that
! gravity makes. That is why this holds the arm at EVERY angle instead of at one angle, and why the
! shoulder is no longer bistable. The attach point is level with the pivot, one notch BELOW the
! link's centre of mass direction, which is what makes the residual stiffness restoring, not runaway.
C SBM D1 1,2,6.5 x -z
R SGY SBM
C EBM R3 3,0,8.5 y -z
R EL EBM
C WBM D1 5,-1,8.5 x -z
R WCAP WBM
E balSh1 SBM ECAP rest=5 k=0.0262
E balSh2 SBM ECAP rest=5 k=0.0262
E balSh3 SBM ECAP rest=5 k=0.0262
E balSh4 SBM ECAP rest=5 k=0.0262
E balEl1 EBM WBM rest=5 k=0.0179
E balEl2 EBM WBM rest=5 k=0.0179
E balEl3 EBM WBM rest=5 k=0.0179
E balWr1 WBM WT rest=5 k=0.0336
E balWr2 WBM WT rest=5 k=0.0336
